# Regression: Set literal `{a, b, c}` lowered to GorgetArray instead of
# GorgetSet, producing silent UB at runtime.
#
# Root cause: the parser produces `Expr::ArrayLiteral` for both
# `[a, b, c]` (Vec) and `{a, b, c}` (Set), with the convention that
# "semantic analysis distinguishes set vs array by context"
# (src/parser/expr.rs:1663-1674). But the IR lowering only built
# GorgetArray — when the declared type was Set[T], the C-emit memcpy'd
# GorgetArray bytes into a Set[T] slot (different layouts) and `s.len()`
# returned a garbage memory-address-like value.
#
# Fix: lower_array_literal checks decl_type_hint at entry; if Set/HashSet,
# dispatches to lower_set_literal_from_array which uses gorget_set_new
# + gorget_set_add with per-element Move + MoveZero discipline (parallel
# to lower_array_literal). Surfaced 2026-05-12 during the post-Tier-2a
# audit of container-literal hint propagation.
#
# Un-dodged 2026-06-19: was `.len()`-only (a "Don't redesign around compiler
# gaps" smell — iteration was broken before gorget_ordered_set_new fix).
# Now exercises iteration directly.
void main():
    Set[int] s = {1, 2, 3}
    print(s.len())          # expected: 3

    # Iterate the set (ordered insertion order)
    int total = 0
    for x in s:
        total = total + x
    print(f"{total}")       # expected: 6  (1+2+3)

    Set[String] strs = {"a", "b", "c", "a"}
    print(strs.len())       # expected: 3 (duplicate "a" deduped)

    # Empty set literal — still must be GorgetSet, not GorgetArray.
    # (Today `{}` lowers as DictLiteral([])); use the explicit form.
    Set[int] empty = Set[int]()
    print(empty.len())      # expected: 0
